WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.013 --> 00:00:03.180
SPEAKER 1: Our next speaker
is something of a rebel,

00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:07.200
I think, in the
scientific community.

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:09.300
William Happer is
professor emeritus

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:12.270
in the Department of Physics
at Princeton University.

00:00:12.270 --> 00:00:13.680
And prior to coming
to Princeton,

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:16.200
he taught at Columbia
University and served

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:20.970
as director of the Columbia
Radiation Laboratory.

00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:23.550
He has some government
experience, some recent

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:25.830
and some from
about 30 years ago.

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:27.720
He served as director
of energy research

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:31.830
in the US Department of
Energy from 1991 to 1993.

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:35.070
And then most recently,
from 2018 to 2019,

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:38.460
he was deputy assistant to the
president and senior director

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:42.510
for emerging technologies at the
White House National Security

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:44.760
Council.

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:47.280
He told me at dinner
last night that he's

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:49.893
more convinced than ever
after his most recent service

00:00:49.893 --> 00:00:51.810
that there is a deep
state and it's much worse

00:00:51.810 --> 00:00:53.000
than it was 30 years ago.

00:00:57.010 --> 00:01:00.220
He's a co-founder of the
nonprofit CO2 Coalition, which

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:03.190
seeks to educate
policymakers and the public

00:01:03.190 --> 00:01:06.460
about the vital contribution
made by carbon dioxide

00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:09.080
to our lives and our economy.

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:14.905
And I just want to say I'm also
in favor of carbon dioxide.

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:21.610
He is a co-founder of
Magnetic Imaging Technologies

00:01:21.610 --> 00:01:24.250
Incorporated, which
specializes in the use

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:30.100
of laser-polarized noble gases
for magnetic resonance imaging.

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:33.160
He also invented the
Sodium Guidestar,

00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:37.330
which is used in astronomical
adaptive optics systems

00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:39.280
to correct for the
degrading effects

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:42.910
of atmospheric turbulence
on imaging resolution.

00:01:42.910 --> 00:01:45.070
And I'm hoping he's
going to tell me

00:01:45.070 --> 00:01:48.220
at least what that means.

00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:50.050
The author of more
than 200 peer-reviewed

00:01:50.050 --> 00:01:52.030
scientific papers, he's
a member of a number

00:01:52.030 --> 00:01:55.090
of professional societies
and associations.

00:01:55.090 --> 00:01:57.880
His topic today is How To
Think About Climate Change.

00:01:57.880 --> 00:01:59.500
Please Welcome Dr.
William Happer.

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:08.660
WILLIAM HAPPER:
Thanks so much, Tim.

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:16.550
Well, I certainly
appreciate the honor

00:02:16.550 --> 00:02:20.780
of speaking to this gathering,
getting to know a little bit

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:24.020
more about Hillsdale
College, which, of course,

00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:27.890
I knew about before,
but not in such detail

00:02:27.890 --> 00:02:32.360
as I've learned over
the last day or two.

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:37.040
The title of my talk is How
To Think About Climate Change.

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:40.160
And let me see if
this clicker works.

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:42.230
I hope it works.

00:02:42.230 --> 00:02:44.780
Good, OK.

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:48.170
So the first thing to--

00:02:48.170 --> 00:02:52.290
the best way to think about
the frenzy over climate

00:02:52.290 --> 00:02:56.870
is it's kind of a modern version
of the medieval crusades.

00:02:56.870 --> 00:03:00.530
You remember that the
motto of the crusaders

00:03:00.530 --> 00:03:06.140
was Deus Vult, "God Wants It."

00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:10.130
And it's hard to pick a
better virtue-signaling slogan

00:03:10.130 --> 00:03:11.510
than that.

00:03:11.510 --> 00:03:16.400
But so far, most of
the climate enthusiasts

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:17.810
have not gone that far.

00:03:17.810 --> 00:03:18.920
Some have, actually.

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:20.930
They claim they're
doing God's work.

00:03:24.190 --> 00:03:26.080
Because of decades
of propaganda,

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:28.600
many Americans,
perhaps including

00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:31.240
some of you here today,
think there really

00:03:31.240 --> 00:03:34.150
is a climate emergency.

00:03:34.150 --> 00:03:40.090
Those who think that way, in
many cases they mean very well.

00:03:40.090 --> 00:03:45.470
But they've been misled by
decades of misinformation.

00:03:45.470 --> 00:03:49.310
So as a scientist who actually
knows a lot about climate,

00:03:49.310 --> 00:03:54.250
and I set out many of our
climate research centers

00:03:54.250 --> 00:03:59.420
when I was at the Department
of Energy in the early 1990s,

00:03:59.420 --> 00:04:03.220
I can assure you that there
is no climate emergency.

00:04:03.220 --> 00:04:07.050
There will not be a
climate emergency.

00:04:07.050 --> 00:04:09.960
And crusades have
always ended badly.

00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:13.470
They brought discredit to
the supposed righteous cause.

00:04:13.470 --> 00:04:16.380
They brought hardship
and death to multitudes.

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:21.690
And policies to address this
phony climate of emergency

00:04:21.690 --> 00:04:25.380
will cause great damage
to American citizens

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:29.290
and to their environment.

00:04:29.290 --> 00:04:30.370
But it's heating up.

00:04:30.370 --> 00:04:37.630
On February 4, Bernie Sanders
and Alexandra Ocasio-Cortez

00:04:37.630 --> 00:04:43.840
and Congressman Blumenauer from
Oregon introduced legislation--

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:46.300
I quote from
Blumenauer's website--

00:04:46.300 --> 00:04:49.180
"legislation mandating
the declaration

00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:51.970
of a national
climate emergency."

00:04:51.970 --> 00:04:53.800
The National Climate
Emergency Act

00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:55.960
directs the president
of the United States

00:04:55.960 --> 00:04:57.970
to declare a national
climate emergency

00:04:57.970 --> 00:05:02.620
and mobilize every resource at
the country's disposal to halt,

00:05:02.620 --> 00:05:06.010
reverse, mitigate, and
prepare for the consequences

00:05:06.010 --> 00:05:08.570
of this climate crisis.

00:05:08.570 --> 00:05:10.600
Well, there isn't
a climate crisis.

00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:12.760
There will not be
a climate crisis.

00:05:12.760 --> 00:05:14.740
This is utter nonsense.

00:05:14.740 --> 00:05:17.590
[APPLAUSE]

00:05:21.870 --> 00:05:25.220
It gets worse when you
get to the state levels,

00:05:25.220 --> 00:05:28.100
where there's a little
less checks and balances.

00:05:28.100 --> 00:05:33.980
These are remarks made
last week by Charles Ismay,

00:05:33.980 --> 00:05:38.900
the undersecretary for climate
change in Massachusetts.

00:05:38.900 --> 00:05:41.780
And it was made to the
Vermont Climate Council.

00:05:41.780 --> 00:05:44.230
So I'll read it to you.

00:05:44.230 --> 00:05:46.190
So let me say that again.

00:05:46.190 --> 00:05:48.770
50% of our emissions
that need to be reduced

00:05:48.770 --> 00:05:52.310
come from you, the person
across the street, the senior

00:05:52.310 --> 00:05:53.930
on fixed income, right.

00:05:53.930 --> 00:05:57.620
There's no bad guy left,
at least in Massachusetts,

00:05:57.620 --> 00:06:01.400
to point the finger at,
to turn the screws on,

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:05.790
and to break their wills
so they stop emitting.

00:06:05.790 --> 00:06:06.290
That's you.

00:06:06.290 --> 00:06:09.350
We have to break
your will, right?

00:06:09.350 --> 00:06:11.900
I can't even say that publicly."

00:06:11.900 --> 00:06:16.430
Well, a few days later,
Mr. Ismay resigned.

00:06:16.430 --> 00:06:18.620
And had he not, his governor
would have fired him.

00:06:21.150 --> 00:06:23.400
But that's the way crusades are.

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:25.860
This is really not a
question of science.

00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:29.430
This is a question of a
secular religion for some.

00:06:29.430 --> 00:06:31.650
It's a question of
money for others.

00:06:31.650 --> 00:06:33.210
It's a question of
power for others.

00:06:33.210 --> 00:06:36.270
But whatever it is,
it's not science.

00:06:36.270 --> 00:06:45.480
So during the medieval
Crusades, part of it

00:06:45.480 --> 00:06:50.170
was against the supposed threat
to the holy sites in Jerusalem.

00:06:50.170 --> 00:06:52.470
But a lot of it was
against local enemies.

00:06:52.470 --> 00:06:56.940
So the medieval Inquisition
really great job

00:06:56.940 --> 00:07:01.350
on the poor Cathars and the
Waldensians of Southern France

00:07:01.350 --> 00:07:04.590
and the Bogomils in the Balkans.

00:07:04.590 --> 00:07:09.150
And climate fanatics don't
know or care any more

00:07:09.150 --> 00:07:11.370
about the science
of climate than

00:07:11.370 --> 00:07:14.310
these medieval
inquisitions knew or cared

00:07:14.310 --> 00:07:17.820
about the teachings of Christ.

00:07:17.820 --> 00:07:21.240
So just about everyone wants
to live in a clean environment.

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:21.740
I do.

00:07:21.740 --> 00:07:23.420
I'm sure everyone here does.

00:07:23.420 --> 00:07:26.930
And this is a photograph--

00:07:26.930 --> 00:07:28.860
I hope it shows
up on the screen.

00:07:28.860 --> 00:07:30.660
I don't see it just yet--

00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:33.050
of Shanghai.

00:07:33.050 --> 00:07:38.960
And that's real air pollution.

00:07:38.960 --> 00:07:41.510
You can just barely see
the bottle-opener building

00:07:41.510 --> 00:07:43.130
in the back through
all the haze.

00:07:43.130 --> 00:07:47.210
And some of this is actually
due to burning coal.

00:07:47.210 --> 00:07:50.420
But a bigger fraction is due
to dust from the Gobi Desert.

00:07:50.420 --> 00:07:53.360
They've had this type
of pollution in Shanghai

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:56.910
since the days of Marco
Polo and long before.

00:07:56.910 --> 00:07:59.850
And part of it is
burning rice fields,

00:07:59.850 --> 00:08:04.160
the stubble in the fields, which
is traditionally done there.

00:08:04.160 --> 00:08:08.120
And this is the kind of
thing that I do not support.

00:08:08.120 --> 00:08:10.280
I wouldn't want to live
in a city like that.

00:08:10.280 --> 00:08:13.520
And if there's anything to
do that will make it better,

00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:16.190
I would certainly support that.

00:08:16.190 --> 00:08:19.940
But none of this has
anything to do with CO2.

00:08:19.940 --> 00:08:26.420
CO2 is a gas you can't
see, smell, or taste.

00:08:26.420 --> 00:08:29.090
So hairbrained schemes
to limit emissions

00:08:29.090 --> 00:08:31.550
of CO2, which is
actually beneficial,

00:08:31.550 --> 00:08:33.140
as I'll explain a
little bit later,

00:08:33.140 --> 00:08:36.080
will only make it harder to
get rid of real pollutants,

00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:40.820
like the pollutant I
showed you in Shanghai.

00:08:40.820 --> 00:08:46.630
So let's talk about CO2.

00:08:46.630 --> 00:08:51.040
Number one, it's not
a pollutant at all.

00:08:51.040 --> 00:08:55.090
We breathe out lots of CO2.

00:08:55.090 --> 00:08:56.650
Many people are
surprised to learn

00:08:56.650 --> 00:09:01.160
that they exhale a little more
than two pounds of CO2 a day.

00:09:01.160 --> 00:09:04.750
So you people in this room
are putting out a lot of CO2.

00:09:04.750 --> 00:09:07.370
I actually brought
a CO2 meter here,

00:09:07.370 --> 00:09:09.460
which I'm going to turn on.

00:09:09.460 --> 00:09:11.440
It takes about 30
seconds to warm up.

00:09:11.440 --> 00:09:13.720
But we will see
what the levels are.

00:09:13.720 --> 00:09:16.000
Before I came down here,
my wife, Barbara, and I

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:19.030
measured the CO2 on
our balcony outside.

00:09:19.030 --> 00:09:22.900
And it's about 400 parts per
million outside this building.

00:09:22.900 --> 00:09:24.160
So this is warming up now.

00:09:24.160 --> 00:09:28.820
I'll show what the results
are here in a minute.

00:09:28.820 --> 00:09:33.130
But our breath is not that
different from the output

00:09:33.130 --> 00:09:34.750
of a power plant.

00:09:34.750 --> 00:09:36.790
Power plants take in
normal air, and they

00:09:36.790 --> 00:09:42.610
burn a fair amount of the oxygen
out of the air in burning coal,

00:09:42.610 --> 00:09:45.160
or natural gas, or oil.

00:09:45.160 --> 00:09:47.890
And the result that
comes out of the stack

00:09:47.890 --> 00:09:51.940
is mostly the nitrogen
that was already there,

00:09:51.940 --> 00:09:54.610
a little bit of oxygen
that wasn't used up,

00:09:54.610 --> 00:09:58.090
and water vapor, and CO2.

00:09:58.090 --> 00:10:01.340
And our breath, except for
having a lot more oxygen--

00:10:01.340 --> 00:10:03.760
so you can give
mouth-to-mouth resuscitation.

00:10:03.760 --> 00:10:06.820
You couldn't do it if your
breath was like a power plant.

00:10:06.820 --> 00:10:08.660
It's very similar
to the power plant.

00:10:08.660 --> 00:10:12.100
It's about 4% CO2, 6% water.

00:10:12.100 --> 00:10:17.260
Power plant has a bit more
CO2 and correspondingly less

00:10:17.260 --> 00:10:19.330
oxygen.

00:10:19.330 --> 00:10:22.810
But our breath is
definitely not a pollutant.

00:10:22.810 --> 00:10:27.250
In fact, our breathing reflex
is entirely determined by CO2.

00:10:27.250 --> 00:10:31.390
It's not determined
by oxygen at all.

00:10:31.390 --> 00:10:34.060
It's not a lack of
oxygen, it's too much CO2

00:10:34.060 --> 00:10:36.070
that makes you exhale.

00:10:36.070 --> 00:10:40.720
This is a picture
of my wife, Barbara,

00:10:40.720 --> 00:10:45.580
in New Jersey on
a new solar farm.

00:10:45.580 --> 00:10:50.470
And we'd just seen how well
solar farms and windmills

00:10:50.470 --> 00:10:51.550
work in Texas.

00:10:51.550 --> 00:10:54.205
[LAUGHTER]

00:10:54.205 --> 00:10:56.200
And they never did
work terribly well.

00:10:56.200 --> 00:10:59.540
But we have to be
grateful for nature.

00:10:59.540 --> 00:11:03.160
She seems to have
a sense of humor

00:11:03.160 --> 00:11:05.590
and has taught us a
good lesson, I hope,

00:11:05.590 --> 00:11:10.330
although people seem
to be slow learners.

00:11:10.330 --> 00:11:17.080
But the problem with renewable
energy-- solar, wind--

00:11:17.080 --> 00:11:18.710
is they take up a lot of space.

00:11:18.710 --> 00:11:21.370
I preferred this field
when it was nice and green

00:11:21.370 --> 00:11:22.720
instead of these weedy panels.

00:11:22.720 --> 00:11:24.430
They're quite weedy now.

00:11:24.430 --> 00:11:28.120
This was soon
after it was built.

00:11:28.120 --> 00:11:30.010
They don't work at all at night.

00:11:30.010 --> 00:11:33.670
So you need something else
to provide electrical power

00:11:33.670 --> 00:11:34.738
at night.

00:11:34.738 --> 00:11:36.280
They don't work if
it's a cloudy day.

00:11:36.280 --> 00:11:40.750
They don't work terribly well in
the winter when the sun is low.

00:11:40.750 --> 00:11:43.420
So it's pure virtue signaling.

00:11:43.420 --> 00:11:46.450
They make no economic sense
unless you've massively

00:11:46.450 --> 00:11:49.870
subsidized by the state
and federal governments.

00:11:49.870 --> 00:11:54.040
Here is an early wind
farm in California.

00:11:54.040 --> 00:11:55.570
It's beginning to
fall to pieces.

00:11:55.570 --> 00:11:59.733
But all wind farms
fall to pieces.

00:11:59.733 --> 00:12:01.150
Nobody quite knows
how we're going

00:12:01.150 --> 00:12:06.760
to dispose of all of this
junk when it no longer works.

00:12:06.760 --> 00:12:09.205
It has much the same
problems as solar.

00:12:11.830 --> 00:12:14.200
When I was a kid,
people didn't sit

00:12:14.200 --> 00:12:16.570
in front of computer
screens all day

00:12:16.570 --> 00:12:19.420
and look at simulations
of the world.

00:12:19.420 --> 00:12:22.570
But you could go outside
and look at the clouds,

00:12:22.570 --> 00:12:24.850
or what the weather was like.

00:12:24.850 --> 00:12:29.260
In those days, people knew that
the wind bloweth as it listeth.

00:12:29.260 --> 00:12:31.150
Sometimes it blows,
sometimes it doesn't.

00:12:31.150 --> 00:12:34.340
And we just saw last week,
it didn't blow very much.

00:12:34.340 --> 00:12:36.070
And when it was
trying to blow, it

00:12:36.070 --> 00:12:40.690
was blowing on iced-up windmill
blades and didn't do any good.

00:12:43.270 --> 00:12:45.490
So renewable energy
is really what

00:12:45.490 --> 00:12:49.030
I would call the inverse
Robin Hood strategy.

00:12:49.030 --> 00:12:52.420
You've robbed from the
poor to give from the rich.

00:12:52.420 --> 00:12:56.860
Utilities are permitted to raise
rates because of the capital

00:12:56.860 --> 00:13:00.580
investments in inefficient,
unreliable renewables.

00:13:00.580 --> 00:13:04.240
They junk fully depreciated
coal, gas, and nuclear plants,

00:13:04.240 --> 00:13:06.190
all of which are
working beautifully,

00:13:06.190 --> 00:13:09.430
producing inexpensive,
reliable energy.

00:13:09.430 --> 00:13:12.850
But regulated profits
are much less.

00:13:12.850 --> 00:13:17.350
So taxpayers subsidize
the rich, who

00:13:17.350 --> 00:13:20.920
can afford to lease land
for wind and solar farms.

00:13:20.920 --> 00:13:23.590
The tax incentives is
pandered to the upper class

00:13:23.590 --> 00:13:25.660
and gated communities
who can afford

00:13:25.660 --> 00:13:29.230
to buy Tesla, electric cars
with subsidies from the state

00:13:29.230 --> 00:13:31.660
and federal government,
often subsidies

00:13:31.660 --> 00:13:36.460
for the electrical power
to charge up their toy.

00:13:36.460 --> 00:13:39.130
And the common people
have little spare income.

00:13:39.130 --> 00:13:42.190
They pay more and more for
the necessities of life

00:13:42.190 --> 00:13:46.560
in order to subsidize
their betters.

00:13:46.560 --> 00:13:52.590
Now, you can't spend a
lifetime as a professor

00:13:52.590 --> 00:13:56.070
and not relapse
from time to time

00:13:56.070 --> 00:13:58.080
to giving a classroom lecture.

00:13:58.080 --> 00:14:01.500
So you will have to
expect to be lectured

00:14:01.500 --> 00:14:03.210
to now for a few minutes.

00:14:03.210 --> 00:14:07.350
And the good thing is
there will be no quiz.

00:14:10.350 --> 00:14:12.360
But as you try to--

00:14:12.360 --> 00:14:16.740
those of you who share my view
that this climate hysteria is

00:14:16.740 --> 00:14:20.320
nonsense, it helps to
know what the facts are.

00:14:20.320 --> 00:14:26.510
So I hope I can arm some of you
with the real scientific facts.

00:14:26.510 --> 00:14:35.820
This is a picture of the overall
circulation of the atmosphere

00:14:35.820 --> 00:14:37.600
around the Earth.

00:14:37.600 --> 00:14:40.690
And the thing that keeps us
warm on Earth is the sun.

00:14:40.690 --> 00:14:42.150
Everybody knows that.

00:14:42.150 --> 00:14:45.270
Things warm up when the
sun comes up at dawn.

00:14:45.270 --> 00:14:49.020
And it's nice and warm,
at least in summertime

00:14:49.020 --> 00:14:50.880
and the early afternoon.

00:14:50.880 --> 00:14:52.710
It cools off at night.

00:14:52.710 --> 00:14:56.820
And the Earth is warm mostly in
the tropics, the reddish area

00:14:56.820 --> 00:14:58.660
on this chart here.

00:14:58.660 --> 00:15:03.180
That's where the sun is most
nearly overhead at noon.

00:15:03.180 --> 00:15:05.520
And so there is
more solar energy

00:15:05.520 --> 00:15:06.900
coming in at the tropics.

00:15:06.900 --> 00:15:10.110
Then it goes out as
cooling radiation to space.

00:15:10.110 --> 00:15:12.900
The sun-- the Earth
also radiates.

00:15:12.900 --> 00:15:17.640
And if you've had dinner
here at the Marriott,

00:15:17.640 --> 00:15:21.240
maybe you've sat under some
of the infrared radiators

00:15:21.240 --> 00:15:22.650
on the outside patios.

00:15:22.650 --> 00:15:25.150
And so you felt the
heat, radiant heat.

00:15:25.150 --> 00:15:27.360
You can't see it, but you
can feel the radiation.

00:15:27.360 --> 00:15:30.060
That's what Earth
radiates out to space.

00:15:30.060 --> 00:15:31.260
So we cool.

00:15:31.260 --> 00:15:34.740
Our Earth cools by radiating
out into very cold space

00:15:34.740 --> 00:15:39.720
and by warming up from
the sunlight shining

00:15:39.720 --> 00:15:43.510
during the half of the day.

00:15:43.510 --> 00:15:47.880
So climate involves a
complicated interplay

00:15:47.880 --> 00:15:51.810
of the sunlight that warms
us, the thermal infraradiation

00:15:51.810 --> 00:15:56.550
that goes out into
space, and the transport

00:15:56.550 --> 00:16:00.030
of this heat by both
radiation and by the motion

00:16:00.030 --> 00:16:02.260
of the air and the water.

00:16:02.260 --> 00:16:05.460
For example, I think we all
know about the Gulf stream that

00:16:05.460 --> 00:16:09.540
carries huge amounts of
heat to northern Europe,

00:16:09.540 --> 00:16:12.450
even to Russia.

00:16:12.450 --> 00:16:15.270
And these currents of
air in the atmosphere

00:16:15.270 --> 00:16:18.720
also carry a lot of heat.

00:16:18.720 --> 00:16:20.820
Since we're in Phoenix,
let me point out

00:16:20.820 --> 00:16:24.750
that if you look there, you
can sort of estimate where

00:16:24.750 --> 00:16:28.150
Phoenix is, just over
the Mexican border.

00:16:28.150 --> 00:16:32.560
It's where the color changes
from yellow to green.

00:16:32.560 --> 00:16:35.880
And this is the descending
branch of that Hadley cell.

00:16:35.880 --> 00:16:39.540
That's this air that rises
up near the equator--

00:16:39.540 --> 00:16:41.600
hot air, moist
air, full of water.

00:16:41.600 --> 00:16:43.990
So tremendous
rainfall down there.

00:16:43.990 --> 00:16:46.800
And as the air
gets really high--

00:16:46.800 --> 00:16:51.060
10, 15 kilometers-- most of
the water has been wrung out.

00:16:51.060 --> 00:16:52.530
It's been freeze-dried out.

00:16:52.530 --> 00:16:54.780
And then it heads
north or south.

00:16:54.780 --> 00:16:57.360
And the air that
heads north eventually

00:16:57.360 --> 00:16:58.920
falls back down to the Earth.

00:16:58.920 --> 00:17:02.580
And typically, it
falls down over Phoenix

00:17:02.580 --> 00:17:07.020
and over 30 degrees north
or 30 degrees south.

00:17:07.020 --> 00:17:09.180
That's the great desert
belts of the Earth.

00:17:09.180 --> 00:17:12.119
That's where the Sahara
is and the Chihuahuan

00:17:12.119 --> 00:17:16.589
Desert and, to the south,
the Kalahari Desert.

00:17:16.589 --> 00:17:19.140
And so these are
regions of Earth

00:17:19.140 --> 00:17:22.920
which are underneath
these cascades of dry air

00:17:22.920 --> 00:17:26.670
that was produced in the
tropics at the equator

00:17:26.670 --> 00:17:28.590
and naturally went north.

00:17:28.590 --> 00:17:31.920
It's a giant gas
turbine, but driven

00:17:31.920 --> 00:17:39.390
by the sun and not spinning
any windmill blades, at least

00:17:39.390 --> 00:17:42.610
at those altitudes.

00:17:42.610 --> 00:17:48.850
Here is a picture of
Earth's energy budget.

00:17:48.850 --> 00:17:50.620
I mentioned we're
warmed by the sun.

00:17:50.620 --> 00:17:55.630
About half of the sunlight
eventually gets to the surface.

00:17:55.630 --> 00:17:58.240
The thing that prevents it
all from reaching the surface

00:17:58.240 --> 00:17:59.050
are clouds.

00:17:59.050 --> 00:18:04.180
Other parts of America,
like New Jersey now,

00:18:04.180 --> 00:18:06.460
are covered with clouds, so
you don't get any sunlight

00:18:06.460 --> 00:18:07.900
directly.

00:18:07.900 --> 00:18:10.150
And some of it is absorbed.

00:18:10.150 --> 00:18:13.480
But the half that does reach
the ground, on average,

00:18:13.480 --> 00:18:14.690
heats the ground.

00:18:14.690 --> 00:18:16.390
And so the ground gets hotter.

00:18:16.390 --> 00:18:18.310
You can notice that
in the afternoon.

00:18:18.310 --> 00:18:21.520
If you go out, and if
you're a gardener like me,

00:18:21.520 --> 00:18:23.330
you can put your
hands in the soil.

00:18:23.330 --> 00:18:24.490
And it's nice and warm.

00:18:24.490 --> 00:18:28.240
It makes the corn grow.

00:18:28.240 --> 00:18:30.040
And that has to be released.

00:18:30.040 --> 00:18:33.080
If you keep piling heat up,
it gets hotter, and hotter,

00:18:33.080 --> 00:18:33.580
and hotter.

00:18:33.580 --> 00:18:36.010
So it's released by
radiation to space, which

00:18:36.010 --> 00:18:39.370
is that red arrow going up.

00:18:39.370 --> 00:18:43.270
So the ground transports
solar heating upward.

00:18:43.270 --> 00:18:45.820
Near the surface, the
first few kilometers,

00:18:45.820 --> 00:18:47.440
a good fraction of
that heat is not

00:18:47.440 --> 00:18:48.940
carried by radiation at all.

00:18:48.940 --> 00:18:53.800
It's completely immune to
anything you do to CO2.

00:18:53.800 --> 00:18:59.200
But once you get up to
10 kilometers or so,

00:18:59.200 --> 00:19:02.980
most of that heat transport
goes into radiation,

00:19:02.980 --> 00:19:05.860
this infrared
radiation I mentioned.

00:19:05.860 --> 00:19:11.830
And by the way, I
have this running now.

00:19:11.830 --> 00:19:14.470
The outside air is 400.

00:19:14.470 --> 00:19:16.770
And I'm not sure
you can see this,

00:19:16.770 --> 00:19:18.070
so I'll read it out to you.

00:19:18.070 --> 00:19:20.950
But it's 580 in here.

00:19:20.950 --> 00:19:24.490
So what this means-- this
is not a whole lot higher

00:19:24.490 --> 00:19:26.830
than the 400 outside.

00:19:26.830 --> 00:19:28.180
During lunch, we had this out.

00:19:28.180 --> 00:19:30.640
And it was 1,000 where
we were having lunch.

00:19:30.640 --> 00:19:35.870
But it makes the point that
CO2 levels are never stable,

00:19:35.870 --> 00:19:36.460
you know?

00:19:36.460 --> 00:19:40.690
People are panicking
about one or two parts

00:19:40.690 --> 00:19:42.010
per million of CO2.

00:19:42.010 --> 00:19:44.590
This is-- oh, now it's at 608.

00:19:44.590 --> 00:19:46.690
That's probably because
I breathed on it.

00:19:46.690 --> 00:19:48.685
[LAUGHTER]

00:19:48.685 --> 00:19:50.110
Hot air sets it off.

00:19:50.110 --> 00:19:54.535
[LAUGHTER]

00:19:54.535 --> 00:19:55.660
These are interesting, too.

00:19:55.660 --> 00:19:58.450
I sometimes take it
out on my back porch.

00:19:58.450 --> 00:20:04.240
And at the end of a summer day,
the CO2 levels on my back porch

00:20:04.240 --> 00:20:07.720
drop to maybe 300, way
below the outside air.

00:20:07.720 --> 00:20:10.500
That's because the trees
and grass in my backyard

00:20:10.500 --> 00:20:13.750
have sucked most of
the CO2 out of the air.

00:20:13.750 --> 00:20:15.730
And then if I get up
early the next morning

00:20:15.730 --> 00:20:18.010
and I look at the
meter, it's up to 600.

00:20:18.010 --> 00:20:22.330
So just from morning to
night, CO2 doubles in the air.

00:20:22.330 --> 00:20:24.760
It doubles and halves,
doubles and halves.

00:20:24.760 --> 00:20:28.060
At least during the growing
season, that's quite common.

00:20:28.060 --> 00:20:32.530
And we have these
hysterics about CO2

00:20:32.530 --> 00:20:35.363
increasing by 30% or 40%.

00:20:35.363 --> 00:20:35.905
It's amazing.

00:20:38.960 --> 00:20:39.460
OK.

00:20:39.460 --> 00:20:42.190
So why the frenzy over CO2?

00:20:42.190 --> 00:20:46.390
The frenzy over CO2 is because
it is a greenhouse gas.

00:20:46.390 --> 00:20:47.140
That is true.

00:20:47.140 --> 00:20:49.690
CO2 is a greenhouse gas.

00:20:49.690 --> 00:20:56.560
This is not-- it's a
somewhat deceptive picture.

00:20:56.560 --> 00:21:03.280
But what it shows
in red is sunlight.

00:21:03.280 --> 00:21:06.100
And the horizontal
scale on that top panel

00:21:06.100 --> 00:21:08.200
is the wavelength
of the sunlight.

00:21:08.200 --> 00:21:16.060
So sunlight is typically about a
half a micron, half a millionth

00:21:16.060 --> 00:21:19.720
of a meter in wavelength.

00:21:19.720 --> 00:21:24.460
That's green light, the
color of green leaves.

00:21:24.460 --> 00:21:27.250
And the thermal radiation
that cools the Earth

00:21:27.250 --> 00:21:35.020
is that blue curve to the
right of the upper panel.

00:21:35.020 --> 00:21:37.000
And that's a much
longer wavelength.

00:21:37.000 --> 00:21:39.160
That's typically
around 10 microns.

00:21:39.160 --> 00:21:42.490
So it's 20 or 30 times
longer wavelengths

00:21:42.490 --> 00:21:45.850
than the wavelengths
from the sun.

00:21:45.850 --> 00:21:49.090
And it turns out
that the sun's energy

00:21:49.090 --> 00:21:52.490
can get through the Earth's
atmosphere very easily.

00:21:52.490 --> 00:21:56.180
So essentially, all
sunlight-- or at least 95%,

00:21:56.180 --> 00:21:59.450
if there are no clouds-- gets
to the surface and warms it.

00:21:59.450 --> 00:22:03.640
But the surface, trying to send
radiation back out to space,

00:22:03.640 --> 00:22:08.560
is unable to get it freely to
space because various gases,

00:22:08.560 --> 00:22:10.550
most importantly, water vapor--

00:22:10.550 --> 00:22:14.800
which is shown as the
third panel down--

00:22:14.800 --> 00:22:17.800
and CO2, which is the
fourth panel down,

00:22:17.800 --> 00:22:19.960
intercept a lot
of that radiation

00:22:19.960 --> 00:22:22.810
and keep it from freely
escaping to space.

00:22:22.810 --> 00:22:28.090
So this keeps Earth's surface
temperature quite a bit

00:22:28.090 --> 00:22:30.700
warmer than it would be,
about 20 or 30 degrees.

00:22:30.700 --> 00:22:33.010
The Earth would be an
ice cube if it were not

00:22:33.010 --> 00:22:35.470
for water vapor and CO2.

00:22:35.470 --> 00:22:38.170
And when I say water vapor, you
should understand that I really

00:22:38.170 --> 00:22:43.540
mean water vapor and clouds,
the condensed form of water.

00:22:43.540 --> 00:22:47.230
So clouds are at least as
important as greenhouse gases.

00:22:47.230 --> 00:22:50.590
And they're very poorly
understood to this day.

00:22:53.680 --> 00:23:00.010
Greenhouse gases were discovered
in the 1850s by this man.

00:23:00.010 --> 00:23:07.600
This is John Tyndall, who was
an Anglo-Irish physicist working

00:23:07.600 --> 00:23:09.230
in London.

00:23:09.230 --> 00:23:11.950
And he was the first
one who discovered

00:23:11.950 --> 00:23:15.880
that water vapor,
or carbon dioxide,

00:23:15.880 --> 00:23:20.680
or ether, or alcohol
intercepts thermal radiation.

00:23:20.680 --> 00:23:28.930
He, as is appropriate
for an Anglo-Irishman,

00:23:28.930 --> 00:23:30.680
his source of heat
was a teakettle.

00:23:30.680 --> 00:23:31.180
[CHUCKLES]

00:23:31.180 --> 00:23:35.150
So he had this hot tea
kettle boiling on a stove.

00:23:35.150 --> 00:23:38.530
And then he had a pipe
through which the radiation

00:23:38.530 --> 00:23:41.710
from the tea kettle could go.

00:23:41.710 --> 00:23:46.690
And he noticed that certain
gases, like CO2 or, especially,

00:23:46.690 --> 00:23:51.430
water vapor, would block
most of that heat radiation,

00:23:51.430 --> 00:23:55.330
whereas the normal atmospheric
gases, nitrogen and oxygen,

00:23:55.330 --> 00:23:56.290
would not.

00:23:56.290 --> 00:24:00.920
So we now call the gases that
block the heat greenhouse

00:24:00.920 --> 00:24:01.420
gases.

00:24:01.420 --> 00:24:04.420
And CO2 is, indeed,
a greenhouse gas.

00:24:04.420 --> 00:24:08.030
It's not a very good greenhouse
gas at today's concentrations.

00:24:08.030 --> 00:24:09.110
So let me show you.

00:24:09.110 --> 00:24:10.960
This is something I
want you to remember.

00:24:10.960 --> 00:24:13.490
This is an important slide.

00:24:13.490 --> 00:24:17.020
So there's a lot
of history here.

00:24:17.020 --> 00:24:21.850
And so there are
two pictures here.

00:24:21.850 --> 00:24:24.370
The top picture's Max Planck.

00:24:24.370 --> 00:24:27.910
And Max Planck was the
great German scientist who

00:24:27.910 --> 00:24:29.890
discovered quantum mechanics.

00:24:29.890 --> 00:24:35.050
And amazingly, quantum mechanics
goes back to greenhouse

00:24:35.050 --> 00:24:37.600
gas physics, thermal
radiation, just what we're

00:24:37.600 --> 00:24:39.550
talking about
today and just what

00:24:39.550 --> 00:24:41.680
climate fanatics
don't understand yet,

00:24:41.680 --> 00:24:43.870
100 years after Planck.

00:24:43.870 --> 00:24:46.870
But Planck understood
it very well.

00:24:46.870 --> 00:24:51.820
And Planck was the
first one who was

00:24:51.820 --> 00:24:57.220
able to show why the
radiation from warm bodies

00:24:57.220 --> 00:24:59.170
has the shape it has.

00:24:59.170 --> 00:25:04.030
On this picture to
the left of Planck

00:25:04.030 --> 00:25:05.120
and below Schwarzschild--

00:25:05.120 --> 00:25:08.680
I'll have more to say about
Schwarzschild in a minute--

00:25:08.680 --> 00:25:10.870
is a smooth blue curve.

00:25:10.870 --> 00:25:13.270
And what that is--
again, left to right

00:25:13.270 --> 00:25:15.940
is wavelength or the
frequency, in this case,

00:25:15.940 --> 00:25:18.190
of light, thermal radiation.

00:25:18.190 --> 00:25:22.760
And up is the thermal power
that's going out to space.

00:25:22.760 --> 00:25:25.930
And so if there were
no greenhouse gases,

00:25:25.930 --> 00:25:29.050
the radiation going to space
would be that Planck curve,

00:25:29.050 --> 00:25:32.030
that blue curve there.

00:25:32.030 --> 00:25:35.860
And that would be the radiation
that keeps the Earth cool.

00:25:35.860 --> 00:25:38.590
In fact, that's not what
you observe if you look down

00:25:38.590 --> 00:25:39.460
from a satellite.

00:25:39.460 --> 00:25:41.920
And we have lots of
measurements now.

00:25:41.920 --> 00:25:43.780
What you see is something
that looks a lot

00:25:43.780 --> 00:25:47.530
like the black curve
that's below with lots

00:25:47.530 --> 00:25:50.240
of jags and wiggles in it.

00:25:50.240 --> 00:25:52.510
And that curve was
first calculated

00:25:52.510 --> 00:25:58.510
by Karl Schwarzschild, whose
picture is below Planck.

00:25:58.510 --> 00:26:01.180
Karl Schwarzschild
was an officer

00:26:01.180 --> 00:26:06.130
in the German army
in World War I.

00:26:06.130 --> 00:26:08.650
And he did some of
his most creative work

00:26:08.650 --> 00:26:13.210
in the trenches on the
Eastern Front, facing Russia.

00:26:13.210 --> 00:26:19.390
And he, in fact, produced one
of the first analytic solutions

00:26:19.390 --> 00:26:22.360
to Einstein's general
theory of relativity

00:26:22.360 --> 00:26:25.270
while he was there
on the front lines.

00:26:25.270 --> 00:26:28.510
And alas, he died
before he got home--

00:26:28.510 --> 00:26:32.780
not from Russian bullets, but
from an autoimmune disease,

00:26:32.780 --> 00:26:35.470
which was really tragic.

00:26:35.470 --> 00:26:40.690
But anyway, Schwarzschild
was the theorist

00:26:40.690 --> 00:26:43.810
who figured out how to calculate
how the real Earth works

00:26:43.810 --> 00:26:44.980
with greenhouse gases.

00:26:44.980 --> 00:26:49.240
And it is those
jagged black lines.

00:26:49.240 --> 00:26:54.100
Now, the important point here
is that you notice there's also

00:26:54.100 --> 00:26:57.130
a red line on this chart.

00:26:57.130 --> 00:26:59.500
What the red line
is is what would

00:26:59.500 --> 00:27:02.830
happen if you doubled the CO2
concentration from today's

00:27:02.830 --> 00:27:04.040
level.

00:27:04.040 --> 00:27:07.220
So right in the middle
of these curves--

00:27:07.220 --> 00:27:07.720
I'm sorry.

00:27:07.720 --> 00:27:09.280
I don't have a
laser pointer here.

00:27:09.280 --> 00:27:12.100
But you can see a gap.

00:27:12.100 --> 00:27:15.940
And that's due to CO2 absorbing
radiation that would otherwise

00:27:15.940 --> 00:27:17.410
cool the Earth.

00:27:17.410 --> 00:27:20.140
If you double the
amount of CO2, you

00:27:20.140 --> 00:27:22.930
don't double the
size of that gap.

00:27:22.930 --> 00:27:25.360
You just go from the black
curve to the red curve,

00:27:25.360 --> 00:27:27.740
and you can barely
see the difference.

00:27:27.740 --> 00:27:30.160
So the message I want
you to understand,

00:27:30.160 --> 00:27:32.320
which practically no
one really understands,

00:27:32.320 --> 00:27:35.780
is that doubling CO2 almost
doesn't make any difference.

00:27:35.780 --> 00:27:38.360
It goes from that black
curve to the red curve.

00:27:38.360 --> 00:27:40.600
And on the basis of
this, we're supposed

00:27:40.600 --> 00:27:41.860
to give up our liberties.

00:27:41.860 --> 00:27:43.720
We're supposed to
give up the gasoline

00:27:43.720 --> 00:27:46.330
engines of our automobiles.

00:27:46.330 --> 00:27:49.900
We're supposed to
accept dictatorial power

00:27:49.900 --> 00:27:54.670
by Bernie Sanders and
Ocasio-Cortez because

00:27:54.670 --> 00:27:57.820
of the difference between
the red and the black curve.

00:27:57.820 --> 00:28:06.100
Don't let anyone convince you
that that is a good bargain.

00:28:06.100 --> 00:28:08.230
It is a terrible bargain.

00:28:08.230 --> 00:28:12.000
The doubling actually does
make a little difference.

00:28:12.000 --> 00:28:14.730
It decreases the
radiation to space

00:28:14.730 --> 00:28:18.090
by about three watts
per square meter.

00:28:18.090 --> 00:28:21.120
In comparison, the
total radiation to space

00:28:21.120 --> 00:28:22.220
is about 300.

00:28:22.220 --> 00:28:23.520
So it's a 1% effect.

00:28:23.520 --> 00:28:25.470
It's actually a
little less than that.

00:28:25.470 --> 00:28:26.820
And that's with no clouds.

00:28:26.820 --> 00:28:30.960
Clouds make everything
even less threatening.

00:28:30.960 --> 00:28:34.140
Finally, let me point out
there is a green curve.

00:28:34.140 --> 00:28:37.080
That's what happens if
you take all the CO2 out

00:28:37.080 --> 00:28:39.390
of the atmosphere.

00:28:39.390 --> 00:28:40.900
No one knows how to do that.

00:28:40.900 --> 00:28:43.380
And of course, we'd
all die because plants

00:28:43.380 --> 00:28:46.890
die if you take all the
CO2 out of the atmosphere.

00:28:46.890 --> 00:28:52.980
But what this curve is telling
you is the greenhouse effect

00:28:52.980 --> 00:28:55.740
of CO2 is already saturated.

00:28:55.740 --> 00:28:57.630
That's the jargon in science.

00:28:57.630 --> 00:29:02.130
It means it's done all
the greenhouse warming

00:29:02.130 --> 00:29:04.350
it can already, practically.

00:29:04.350 --> 00:29:07.113
And adding, doubling
it practically

00:29:07.113 --> 00:29:08.280
doesn't make any difference.

00:29:08.280 --> 00:29:09.360
You could quadruple it.

00:29:09.360 --> 00:29:11.820
It also practically
makes no difference.

00:29:11.820 --> 00:29:16.410
So the CO2 effects are
strongly, strongly saturated.

00:29:20.460 --> 00:29:23.655
Now let me move on.

00:29:30.040 --> 00:29:33.482
You can take that tiny
difference between those curves

00:29:33.482 --> 00:29:35.440
that I showed you, the
red and the black curve,

00:29:35.440 --> 00:29:39.010
and calculate the warming
that should happen.

00:29:39.010 --> 00:29:42.140
And I was one of the first to
do this, actually, in 1982.

00:29:42.140 --> 00:29:45.620
I was the co-author of
one of the first books.

00:29:45.620 --> 00:29:48.910
And you can see
on the right panel

00:29:48.910 --> 00:29:52.510
there is my calculation and lots
of other people's calculations

00:29:52.510 --> 00:29:53.530
since.

00:29:53.530 --> 00:29:57.760
It's a bar graph of
the warming per decade

00:29:57.760 --> 00:30:00.280
that people have calculated.

00:30:00.280 --> 00:30:04.720
The red bar is what's
actually observed.

00:30:04.720 --> 00:30:10.120
And on the right is,
I think, for 10 years.

00:30:10.120 --> 00:30:12.200
And on the left is 20 years.

00:30:12.200 --> 00:30:14.200
But in both cases,
the takeaway message

00:30:14.200 --> 00:30:19.420
is the predicted warming, which
everybody is frantic about,

00:30:19.420 --> 00:30:23.830
are all grossly over-predicting
the warming compared

00:30:23.830 --> 00:30:26.980
to observation, which
is the red bars.

00:30:26.980 --> 00:30:31.960
So the observed warmings have
been extremely small compared

00:30:31.960 --> 00:30:38.710
to computer calculations over
any interval that you look.

00:30:38.710 --> 00:30:41.710
So it's clear that
all of our policies

00:30:41.710 --> 00:30:44.980
are based on the models that
you see here that don't work.

00:30:44.980 --> 00:30:47.090
The models don't work.

00:30:47.090 --> 00:30:50.680
And I think we sort of
know why they don't work,

00:30:50.680 --> 00:30:54.490
but no one is
willing to admit it.

00:30:54.490 --> 00:30:56.050
Here is-- let's see.

00:30:56.050 --> 00:31:01.180
Yeah, here's another
demonstration, again, of that.

00:31:01.180 --> 00:31:03.190
The red curve here--

00:31:03.190 --> 00:31:05.170
on the bottom is the year.

00:31:05.170 --> 00:31:09.130
So I think it starts
in 1975, approximately.

00:31:09.130 --> 00:31:14.080
And it goes through 1925 because
some of them are predictions.

00:31:14.080 --> 00:31:17.890
So the top curve is predictions
by the international climate

00:31:17.890 --> 00:31:19.930
alarm establishment.

00:31:19.930 --> 00:31:25.990
And the bottom curves, the
blue and the green curves,

00:31:25.990 --> 00:31:28.990
are observations.

00:31:28.990 --> 00:31:39.970
And so the blue circles
are measured by balloons.

00:31:39.970 --> 00:31:43.630
You probably know that, every
day, thousands of balloons

00:31:43.630 --> 00:31:48.025
are launched around the world
in North America, Europe, Asia,

00:31:48.025 --> 00:31:49.340
the southern hemisphere.

00:31:49.340 --> 00:31:51.740
And they measure the
temperature as you go up.

00:31:51.740 --> 00:31:54.220
So we know what the
temperature of the atmosphere

00:31:54.220 --> 00:31:57.820
has been doing from these
really good balloon records.

00:31:57.820 --> 00:32:04.600
And it's the blue curves.

00:32:04.600 --> 00:32:08.170
And the warming observed from
balloons is about a third

00:32:08.170 --> 00:32:12.400
of the warming that has
been predicted by models.

00:32:12.400 --> 00:32:14.710
And let me also
add, nobody knows

00:32:14.710 --> 00:32:18.820
how much of this warming
that's observed is due to CO2.

00:32:18.820 --> 00:32:21.340
There's good reason
to think most of it

00:32:21.340 --> 00:32:24.430
would be there even
without an increase in CO2,

00:32:24.430 --> 00:32:28.690
because we're coming out
of the long Little Ice Age.

00:32:28.690 --> 00:32:32.600
And we've been coming out of
that since the early 1800s,

00:32:32.600 --> 00:32:35.980
where it got much, much colder.

00:32:35.980 --> 00:32:39.940
And the green curve is
measurements from satellites.

00:32:39.940 --> 00:32:43.330
Very much like the measurement
of your temporal scanning

00:32:43.330 --> 00:32:46.240
thermometer, you can look
down from a satellite

00:32:46.240 --> 00:32:49.550
and measure the temperature
of the atmosphere.

00:32:49.550 --> 00:32:53.710
So the satellites and the
balloons agree with each other.

00:32:53.710 --> 00:32:55.600
And they do not agree
with the models.

00:32:55.600 --> 00:32:59.890
This is nice work by John
Christy at the University

00:32:59.890 --> 00:33:03.460
of Alabama, Huntsville.

00:33:03.460 --> 00:33:03.960
OK.

00:33:03.960 --> 00:33:11.160
So the alleged harm from
CO2 is from warming.

00:33:11.160 --> 00:33:14.370
And the warming observed
is much, much less

00:33:14.370 --> 00:33:15.110
than predictions.

00:33:15.110 --> 00:33:18.180
In fact, if the warming is
as small as we're observing,

00:33:18.180 --> 00:33:19.890
it's almost
certainly beneficial.

00:33:19.890 --> 00:33:22.740
It gives a little bit
longer growing seasons.

00:33:22.740 --> 00:33:26.520
You can ripen crops a
little bit further north

00:33:26.520 --> 00:33:28.860
than you could before.

00:33:28.860 --> 00:33:32.100
And so it's completely good
news in terms of the temperature

00:33:32.100 --> 00:33:33.450
directly.

00:33:33.450 --> 00:33:36.150
But there's even better news.

00:33:36.150 --> 00:33:39.510
Because in reality, by
historical standards,

00:33:39.510 --> 00:33:42.190
we're living in a
CO2 famine today.

00:33:42.190 --> 00:33:45.370
So let me show you that.

00:33:45.370 --> 00:33:49.020
So this is a picture
of our best estimates

00:33:49.020 --> 00:33:51.840
of past levels of CO2.

00:33:51.840 --> 00:33:56.220
So the vertical scale,
RCO2, is the amount of--

00:33:56.220 --> 00:34:00.130
that CO2 in the past was
greater than it is today.

00:34:00.130 --> 00:34:03.570
And so you can see on that scale
that over most of the past,

00:34:03.570 --> 00:34:08.639
CO2 levels have been five times,
10 times, even 20 times greater

00:34:08.639 --> 00:34:09.810
than today.

00:34:09.810 --> 00:34:11.670
And during this entire period--

00:34:11.670 --> 00:34:16.230
this is the so-called
Phanerozoic,

00:34:16.230 --> 00:34:19.690
since the Cambrian era when
we have good fossil records.

00:34:19.690 --> 00:34:21.389
So we know pretty
well what life was

00:34:21.389 --> 00:34:23.940
doing during that time
from the sediments.

00:34:23.940 --> 00:34:28.350
And all of this period with
much higher CO2 levels,

00:34:28.350 --> 00:34:29.730
life flourished on Earth.

00:34:29.730 --> 00:34:33.719
And in general, it flourished
better when there was more CO2.

00:34:33.719 --> 00:34:38.610
So plants really would prefer
to have two, three, four times

00:34:38.610 --> 00:34:41.239
more CO2 than we have today.

00:34:41.239 --> 00:34:44.429
And you can see plants
already responding to that.

00:34:44.429 --> 00:34:47.650
Let me show you an example.

00:34:47.650 --> 00:34:52.179
This is the greening of the
Earth measured from satellite.

00:34:52.179 --> 00:34:57.000
So this picture shows
areas of the Earth that

00:34:57.000 --> 00:35:04.177
are getting greener over
a 20-year period, I think.

00:35:04.177 --> 00:35:05.760
My eyes aren't good
enough to read it,

00:35:05.760 --> 00:35:08.200
but it's roughly 20 years.

00:35:08.200 --> 00:35:12.010
And what you notice
is that everywhere,

00:35:12.010 --> 00:35:14.370
especially in arid
areas, the Sahel--

00:35:14.370 --> 00:35:17.280
you can see just
south of the Sahara,

00:35:17.280 --> 00:35:19.560
it's greening dramatically.

00:35:19.560 --> 00:35:21.690
The Western United
States is greening.

00:35:21.690 --> 00:35:24.390
Western Australia is greening.

00:35:24.390 --> 00:35:26.580
Western India is greening.

00:35:26.580 --> 00:35:30.870
And this is almost
certainly due to CO2.

00:35:30.870 --> 00:35:36.570
And the reason this
happens is that CO2

00:35:36.570 --> 00:35:42.750
allows plants to grow where
50 years ago, it was too dry.

00:35:42.750 --> 00:35:46.020
So plants are now
needing less water

00:35:46.020 --> 00:35:51.060
to grow than they did
50 or 100 years before.

00:35:51.060 --> 00:35:56.130
Let me show you another example
of what more CO2 does in terms

00:35:56.130 --> 00:35:57.510
of making plants grow better.

00:35:57.510 --> 00:36:02.520
This is a picture of
Dr. Sherwood Idso.

00:36:02.520 --> 00:36:06.840
And it was actually an
experiment done here in Phoenix

00:36:06.840 --> 00:36:08.670
back in the '80s.

00:36:08.670 --> 00:36:13.650
And so this is a pine tree.

00:36:13.650 --> 00:36:18.810
I think it's a Mediterranean
pine, eldarica pine.

00:36:18.810 --> 00:36:20.730
And on the left,
you'll see a pine tree

00:36:20.730 --> 00:36:22.950
growing in the
current CO2 levels

00:36:22.950 --> 00:36:26.010
at that time, which
was about three--

00:36:26.010 --> 00:36:30.090
ambient was 380,
I believe it says.

00:36:30.090 --> 00:36:34.560
And on the right are
more and more CO2.

00:36:34.560 --> 00:36:38.760
So you can see that the pine
trees, the more CO2 you put

00:36:38.760 --> 00:36:41.850
in them, the faster they grow.

00:36:41.850 --> 00:36:45.090
And you can do this
with almost any plant.

00:36:45.090 --> 00:36:52.200
The corn, wheat, cotton, they
all grow better with more CO2.

00:36:52.200 --> 00:36:54.180
So this is the
so-called pollutant

00:36:54.180 --> 00:36:58.770
that you hear about with
the climate emergency.

00:36:58.770 --> 00:37:02.800
So let me explain the
basics of why that works.

00:37:02.800 --> 00:37:08.430
So if you look
closely at a plant,

00:37:08.430 --> 00:37:12.850
you take a low-power
magnifying glass,

00:37:12.850 --> 00:37:15.970
you'll see the leaf was
full of little holes.

00:37:15.970 --> 00:37:19.560
And the little holes are to
let carbon dioxide diffuse

00:37:19.560 --> 00:37:25.500
in from the air into the
moist interior of the leaf,

00:37:25.500 --> 00:37:27.720
where the leaf--

00:37:27.720 --> 00:37:31.620
with the special
enzyme called rubisco,

00:37:31.620 --> 00:37:34.860
one of the most ancient enzymes
in the world and the most

00:37:34.860 --> 00:37:37.830
abundant protein in the world--

00:37:37.830 --> 00:37:42.360
combines the CO2 with
water to make sugar.

00:37:42.360 --> 00:37:45.120
And the energy to run
this little factory,

00:37:45.120 --> 00:37:48.930
this chemical factory within the
leaf, is provided by sunlight.

00:37:48.930 --> 00:37:53.970
So now, the problem with
this is if you see this hole,

00:37:53.970 --> 00:37:56.430
not only is CO2
diffusing in, but there's

00:37:56.430 --> 00:37:59.040
a lot more water
vapor inside the leaf

00:37:59.040 --> 00:38:01.800
than there is CO2
in the air outside.

00:38:01.800 --> 00:38:05.250
So for every CO2 molecule
that diffuses into the leaf,

00:38:05.250 --> 00:38:08.470
there are a hundred water
molecules that diffuse out.

00:38:08.470 --> 00:38:11.460
So the plant has this
engineering dilemma.

00:38:11.460 --> 00:38:14.070
It has to have holes
in its leaf to get

00:38:14.070 --> 00:38:16.440
the CO2 that it needs to live.

00:38:16.440 --> 00:38:19.590
But those same
holes desiccate it.

00:38:19.590 --> 00:38:20.880
They dry it out.

00:38:20.880 --> 00:38:24.090
And it, of course, needs
water to live, too.

00:38:24.090 --> 00:38:27.630
So what is happening all over
the world now is plants grow.

00:38:27.630 --> 00:38:29.200
They are not stupid.

00:38:29.200 --> 00:38:31.410
They grow leaves with
fewer holes in them.

00:38:31.410 --> 00:38:34.470
And so there's more
CO2 in the air outside,

00:38:34.470 --> 00:38:35.940
so they don't need
as many holes.

00:38:35.940 --> 00:38:38.380
But they don't leak
as much water either.

00:38:38.380 --> 00:38:40.830
So that's why you're seeing
the greening of the Earth.

00:38:40.830 --> 00:38:43.230
It's from the plants
themselves taking

00:38:43.230 --> 00:38:47.100
advantage of CO2 coming
back to more historically

00:38:47.100 --> 00:38:50.460
normal levels.

00:38:50.460 --> 00:38:52.410
There's more to say about this.

00:38:52.410 --> 00:38:54.280
There's a second
important issue,

00:38:54.280 --> 00:38:58.650
which is that the
enzyme I mentioned--

00:38:58.650 --> 00:39:02.340
it's called rubisco--
it's very old.

00:39:02.340 --> 00:39:06.700
It was probably invented
on the evolutionary scale 3

00:39:06.700 --> 00:39:10.050
and 1/2 billion years
ago, so extremely ancient.

00:39:10.050 --> 00:39:13.740
And at that time, there
was no oxygen in the air.

00:39:13.740 --> 00:39:16.530
And so this poor
enzyme was designed

00:39:16.530 --> 00:39:20.670
in a way that makes
it poisoned by oxygen.

00:39:20.670 --> 00:39:23.910
And so plants today have a
hard time when there's not

00:39:23.910 --> 00:39:25.830
enough CO2 in the air.

00:39:25.830 --> 00:39:30.120
This enzyme, which is charged
to make sugar, instead of making

00:39:30.120 --> 00:39:34.080
sugar from CO2, it
grabs an oxygen.

00:39:34.080 --> 00:39:36.600
And it turns the
oxygen into things

00:39:36.600 --> 00:39:40.440
like hydrogen peroxide and
other nasty oxidizing things.

00:39:40.440 --> 00:39:42.720
You've heard of
antioxidants in your tea.

00:39:42.720 --> 00:39:44.680
They're to handle this problem.

00:39:44.680 --> 00:39:46.860
That's why they're generated.

00:39:46.860 --> 00:39:51.240
And so if you have more
CO2, this photorespiration

00:39:51.240 --> 00:39:53.565
is the jargon that
goes with that.

00:39:53.565 --> 00:39:54.750
That is suppressed.

00:39:54.750 --> 00:39:57.960
And so plants grow a
lot better with that.

00:39:57.960 --> 00:40:02.310
There's a special type of
plant called C4 plants, which

00:40:02.310 --> 00:40:04.920
includes American
corn and sugarcane,

00:40:04.920 --> 00:40:07.050
that has partially
solved that problem.

00:40:07.050 --> 00:40:10.020
But they don't compete well
with the old-fashioned plants.

00:40:10.020 --> 00:40:13.230
As the CO2 level goes up,
the old-fashioned ones

00:40:13.230 --> 00:40:19.290
without the adaptation
outcompete these C4 plants.

00:40:19.290 --> 00:40:23.130
Well, you often-- when
you raise these issues,

00:40:23.130 --> 00:40:30.930
the hard scientific issues with
your opponents, the fanatics,

00:40:30.930 --> 00:40:36.540
the response is, how can you
say that 97% of scientists

00:40:36.540 --> 00:40:39.720
agree that there is a
terrible emergency here

00:40:39.720 --> 00:40:42.100
that we have to cope with?

00:40:42.100 --> 00:40:47.670
Let's see if I can
get this back again.

00:40:47.670 --> 00:40:52.790
And there are several
things you should say.

00:40:52.790 --> 00:41:00.310
You should say, first of all, in
science, truth is not voted on.

00:41:00.310 --> 00:41:03.170
It's not like voting on a law.

00:41:03.170 --> 00:41:08.610
It's determined by how well your
theory agrees with observations

00:41:08.610 --> 00:41:09.650
and experiments.

00:41:09.650 --> 00:41:12.080
I just showed you that
the theories of warming

00:41:12.080 --> 00:41:13.210
are grossly wrong.

00:41:13.210 --> 00:41:14.670
They're not even close.

00:41:14.670 --> 00:41:17.630
And yet we're making
our policy decisions

00:41:17.630 --> 00:41:21.020
based on these
theories, these computer

00:41:21.020 --> 00:41:22.850
models that don't work.

00:41:22.850 --> 00:41:26.060
So that's one point, is that it
doesn't matter how many people

00:41:26.060 --> 00:41:27.590
say there's an emergency.

00:41:27.590 --> 00:41:30.290
If it doesn't agree with
the experiment, it's wrong.

00:41:30.290 --> 00:41:32.650
And it's definitely wrong.

00:41:32.650 --> 00:41:36.830
The second thing is even
when people agree in science,

00:41:36.830 --> 00:41:40.770
people think of
scientists as somehow

00:41:40.770 --> 00:41:47.350
these incorruptible,
priestly people.

00:41:47.350 --> 00:41:48.350
They're not that at all.

00:41:48.350 --> 00:41:51.380
They have all the same
faults as everybody else.

00:41:51.380 --> 00:41:52.930
And they're frequently wrong.

00:41:52.930 --> 00:41:55.250
A good example of that
was continental drift.

00:41:55.250 --> 00:42:02.360
When I was a
student in the '50s,

00:42:02.360 --> 00:42:06.560
nobody with any
self-respect in geology

00:42:06.560 --> 00:42:08.510
admitted to believing
in continental drift,

00:42:08.510 --> 00:42:11.660
although it had been
proposed by Alfred

00:42:11.660 --> 00:42:16.970
Wegener, a German scientist
in the '20s and '30s.

00:42:16.970 --> 00:42:19.880
And it was based on very
good, hard evidence.

00:42:19.880 --> 00:42:23.720
An example is shown below.

00:42:23.720 --> 00:42:26.300
The fact, for example, that
Africa, and South America,

00:42:26.300 --> 00:42:30.710
and India, and Antarctica
fit together like a jigsaw.

00:42:30.710 --> 00:42:32.760
So you've taken a jigsaw apart.

00:42:32.760 --> 00:42:37.370
So that's pretty
suggestive, I would say.

00:42:37.370 --> 00:42:42.230
And secondly, if you look at
the fossils in these continents

00:42:42.230 --> 00:42:45.450
from 300 million years
ago, 400 million years ago

00:42:45.450 --> 00:42:49.400
today, they go smoothly from
South America to Africa,

00:42:49.400 --> 00:42:53.600
or from Africa to India,
or even into Antarctica,

00:42:53.600 --> 00:42:56.360
that you find fossils
of the same organisms

00:42:56.360 --> 00:42:58.970
even though there's
thousands of kilometers

00:42:58.970 --> 00:43:01.620
of sea separating them today.

00:43:01.620 --> 00:43:05.390
And so that, again, suggests
that these were connected.

00:43:05.390 --> 00:43:10.700
But nevertheless, in spite of
this quite persuasive evidence,

00:43:10.700 --> 00:43:14.190
Wegener was scorned and
mocked all his life.

00:43:14.190 --> 00:43:17.420
He died falling into a
crevasse in Greenland,

00:43:17.420 --> 00:43:20.900
exploring, looking for fossils.

00:43:20.900 --> 00:43:22.730
It's sort of a sad ending.

00:43:22.730 --> 00:43:24.480
But he was absolutely right.

00:43:24.480 --> 00:43:28.700
And the clincher actually
came when we finally

00:43:28.700 --> 00:43:34.820
declassified the World War II
North Atlantic magnetic anomaly

00:43:34.820 --> 00:43:39.140
data, which we had been sitting
on for 10 years, which showed

00:43:39.140 --> 00:43:44.240
a conveyor belt of crust
being formed starting

00:43:44.240 --> 00:43:47.900
mid-Atlantic region
and going out,

00:43:47.900 --> 00:43:51.140
left and right, toward
America, toward Europe.

00:43:51.140 --> 00:43:52.640
So there was
absolutely no question

00:43:52.640 --> 00:43:54.890
that the sea floor was
splitting, spreading.

00:43:54.890 --> 00:43:58.460
That's the one bit of evidence
that Wegener did not have.

00:43:58.460 --> 00:44:00.110
But he had lots
of other evidence

00:44:00.110 --> 00:44:02.340
that should have
persuaded people.

00:44:02.340 --> 00:44:04.200
So this is just one example.

00:44:04.200 --> 00:44:07.700
I could tell you many others
of scientific consensuses

00:44:07.700 --> 00:44:08.870
that made no sense.

00:44:08.870 --> 00:44:12.590
This one is interesting because
it had no political background.

00:44:12.590 --> 00:44:14.150
It was pure science.

00:44:14.150 --> 00:44:17.360
But it does illustrate
the fallibility

00:44:17.360 --> 00:44:21.620
of scientists and the
groupthink that goes in science.

00:44:21.620 --> 00:44:24.890
If you wanted to advance
as a young geologist,

00:44:24.890 --> 00:44:28.100
you could write a paper
scorning Wegener in 1950

00:44:28.100 --> 00:44:31.880
and get promoted right away,
even though your paper was

00:44:31.880 --> 00:44:33.050
completely wrong.

00:44:33.050 --> 00:44:35.834
And once you get tenure,
you're there for good.

00:44:35.834 --> 00:44:36.334
[LAUGHS]

00:44:36.334 --> 00:44:37.445
[LAUGHTER]

00:44:37.445 --> 00:44:42.090
So let me-- this is probably
a little too long a lecture.

00:44:42.090 --> 00:44:44.180
So let me bring it to a close.

00:44:44.180 --> 00:44:46.700
And I'll take some questions.

00:44:46.700 --> 00:44:51.710
So the takeaway message
is that the policies

00:44:51.710 --> 00:44:55.850
to slow CO2 emissions, they're
based on flawed computer models

00:44:55.850 --> 00:44:59.360
which exaggerate warming
by factors of two or three,

00:44:59.360 --> 00:45:01.700
probably more.

00:45:01.700 --> 00:45:02.840
That's message number one.

00:45:02.840 --> 00:45:05.062
So why do we give
up our freedom?

00:45:05.062 --> 00:45:06.770
What do we do we give
up our automobiles?

00:45:06.770 --> 00:45:10.010
Why do we give up a beefsteak
because of this model

00:45:10.010 --> 00:45:11.750
that doesn't work?

00:45:11.750 --> 00:45:15.050
And well, at number two,
it's that if you really

00:45:15.050 --> 00:45:18.480
look into it, more CO2
actually benefits the world.

00:45:18.480 --> 00:45:20.330
So what are we doing?

00:45:20.330 --> 00:45:23.870
This beneficial molecule
that's making plants

00:45:23.870 --> 00:45:27.335
grow better, that's giving us
a little less harsh winters,

00:45:27.335 --> 00:45:30.800
a slightly longer
growing season,

00:45:30.800 --> 00:45:32.370
why is that a pollutant?

00:45:32.370 --> 00:45:33.680
It's not a pollutant at all.

00:45:33.680 --> 00:45:36.440
And we should have the
courage to do nothing

00:45:36.440 --> 00:45:37.610
about CO2 emissions.

00:45:37.610 --> 00:45:39.380
Nothing needs to be done.

00:45:39.380 --> 00:45:39.930
So thank you.

00:45:39.930 --> 00:45:41.813
I'll stop at this point, yep.

00:45:41.813 --> 00:45:47.838
[APPLAUSE]

00:45:52.185 --> 00:45:53.560
SPEAKER 2: Thank
you, Dr. Happer.

00:45:53.560 --> 00:45:56.300
We now have time for some
Q&amp;A. If you have a question,

00:45:56.300 --> 00:45:58.300
please raise your hand
and wait for a microphone

00:45:58.300 --> 00:45:59.175
to be brought to you.

00:46:04.812 --> 00:46:08.740
AUDIENCE: It's a very
interesting conversation.

00:46:08.740 --> 00:46:12.960
One of the things that the
environmentalists always forget

00:46:12.960 --> 00:46:15.330
is the tremendous
amount of CO2 that

00:46:15.330 --> 00:46:20.490
is produced to get
the raw materials,

00:46:20.490 --> 00:46:24.537
to make the wind turbines,
to make the solar cells.

00:46:24.537 --> 00:46:26.370
But another thing that
I have thought about,

00:46:26.370 --> 00:46:29.190
and I've never
seen it discussed--

00:46:29.190 --> 00:46:33.360
every one of those things takes
heat out of the atmosphere.

00:46:33.360 --> 00:46:37.090
Has anybody ever
figured that out?

00:46:37.090 --> 00:46:40.500
The second thing is we talk
about the rise of the ocean,

00:46:40.500 --> 00:46:42.540
of the sea level.

00:46:42.540 --> 00:46:47.580
Everything we put into the
ocean raises the sea level

00:46:47.580 --> 00:46:48.450
a little bit.

00:46:48.450 --> 00:46:51.420
And all of the mud
that flows off the land

00:46:51.420 --> 00:46:53.340
raises the sea level.

00:46:53.340 --> 00:46:56.160
They think it just comes
out from glacier melting,

00:46:56.160 --> 00:47:00.900
but there's much more
complicated than that.

00:47:00.900 --> 00:47:03.690
WILLIAM HAPPER: Yeah, thank you.

00:47:03.690 --> 00:47:07.860
It's true that anything you
make in our modern world

00:47:07.860 --> 00:47:10.950
involves CO2 production or
sometimes that carbon actually

00:47:10.950 --> 00:47:11.970
goes into the product.

00:47:11.970 --> 00:47:14.340
Plastics are made of carbon.

00:47:14.340 --> 00:47:16.230
My coat is made of carbon.

00:47:19.350 --> 00:47:23.580
With respect to--
the amount of that,

00:47:23.580 --> 00:47:28.350
I think it's not negligible.

00:47:28.350 --> 00:47:33.510
But the biggest source of CO2
is actually burning fossil fuels

00:47:33.510 --> 00:47:36.670
to get energy for
transportation, mostly,

00:47:36.670 --> 00:47:38.700
but also electrical power.

00:47:38.700 --> 00:47:42.720
Another non-trivial
part is cement-making.

00:47:42.720 --> 00:47:45.670
The way you make cement
is you take limestone,

00:47:45.670 --> 00:47:51.210
which is calcium carbonate, and
you have to burn oil or coal.

00:47:51.210 --> 00:47:52.570
And you bake that out.

00:47:52.570 --> 00:47:55.470
And so you get the CO2 from
burning the oil or coal

00:47:55.470 --> 00:47:57.600
to heat it, to burn the lime.

00:47:57.600 --> 00:48:00.690
And then when you're burning
it, CO2 is coming out.

00:48:00.690 --> 00:48:03.090
So what you're doing
is you're baking

00:48:03.090 --> 00:48:05.370
CO2 out of the limestone.

00:48:05.370 --> 00:48:09.510
So you got a sort of double
whammy from cement manufacture.

00:48:09.510 --> 00:48:11.190
I wouldn't call it
a double whammy.

00:48:11.190 --> 00:48:14.620
I would call it a double
benefit because, as I said,

00:48:14.620 --> 00:48:18.270
it's really good
for life on Earth.

00:48:18.270 --> 00:48:20.100
Plants love more CO2.

00:48:20.100 --> 00:48:21.870
We're happy with more CO2, too.

00:48:21.870 --> 00:48:23.950
We know a lot about
how it affects humans.

00:48:23.950 --> 00:48:26.280
It doesn't affect us at all.

00:48:26.280 --> 00:48:31.560
As for sea level, sea levels
started to rise around 1800,

00:48:31.560 --> 00:48:34.260
at the end of the
Little Ice Age.

00:48:34.260 --> 00:48:37.500
And they've been rising
steadily at about 2 millimeters

00:48:37.500 --> 00:48:39.180
per year.

00:48:39.180 --> 00:48:40.590
2 millimeters is not very much.

00:48:40.590 --> 00:48:42.300
You can barely see it.

00:48:42.300 --> 00:48:47.550
Well, that's maybe
9 inches a century.

00:48:47.550 --> 00:48:50.130
If you go to the sea, a
typical tide is quite a bit

00:48:50.130 --> 00:48:52.065
more than 9 inches.

00:48:52.065 --> 00:48:54.840
So if 9 inches is
going to bother you,

00:48:54.840 --> 00:48:58.360
you've been swindled in the
real estate you've got there.

00:48:58.360 --> 00:49:04.410
So nobody knows how long
this rise will continue.

00:49:04.410 --> 00:49:07.710
It could easily go
for another century.

00:49:07.710 --> 00:49:10.620
Or it could stop in
a couple of years.

00:49:10.620 --> 00:49:13.020
One, I was having
lunch with someone.

00:49:13.020 --> 00:49:16.320
And they asked, what's
interesting in basic science?

00:49:16.320 --> 00:49:18.240
And one of the more
interesting things

00:49:18.240 --> 00:49:21.840
is speculation is
that solar activity is

00:49:21.840 --> 00:49:23.280
beginning to decrease.

00:49:23.280 --> 00:49:27.960
If you look at sunspot
activity, it's definitely less

00:49:27.960 --> 00:49:29.220
the last few years.

00:49:29.220 --> 00:49:33.660
And we've had these rather cold
winters the last few years.

00:49:33.660 --> 00:49:39.100
And one of the possible drivers
of climate is solar activity.

00:49:39.100 --> 00:49:42.060
So if there really is going
to be a cooling period--

00:49:42.060 --> 00:49:45.570
we had a cooling period,
for example, in the '70s.

00:49:45.570 --> 00:49:48.960
I remember, then, reading
about the coming Ice

00:49:48.960 --> 00:49:50.700
Age in Newsweek and Time.

00:49:50.700 --> 00:49:54.930
And they flipped
immediately 10 years later.

00:49:54.930 --> 00:49:58.260
So in physics, we have
a joke which says,

00:49:58.260 --> 00:50:00.930
the hardest thing about a theory
is getting the sign right.

00:50:00.930 --> 00:50:02.070
And that's been true of--

00:50:02.070 --> 00:50:03.390
[LAUGHTER]

00:50:03.390 --> 00:50:06.690
--that's been true of
climate for a long time.

00:50:06.690 --> 00:50:10.090
Anyway, it could cool in
another 10 or 15 years.

00:50:10.090 --> 00:50:11.340
I don't have a strong opinion.

00:50:11.340 --> 00:50:12.382
But I'm interested in it.

00:50:15.485 --> 00:50:19.140
SPEAKER 3: We have a question
on the speaker's right.

00:50:19.140 --> 00:50:22.500
AUDIENCE: I understand
that the original idea was

00:50:22.500 --> 00:50:27.420
that CO2 would cause a
slight warming, which

00:50:27.420 --> 00:50:33.510
would cause more water vapor,
which was the big greenhouse

00:50:33.510 --> 00:50:38.700
gas, which would cause still
more water vapor and more

00:50:38.700 --> 00:50:42.750
heating, et cetera, and
a runaway situation.

00:50:42.750 --> 00:50:44.430
The fact that the Earth--

00:50:44.430 --> 00:50:47.880
well, that the CO2,
its only effect

00:50:47.880 --> 00:50:52.717
was to increase the temperature
and start that cycle,

00:50:52.717 --> 00:50:54.300
shouldn't it have
been kind of obvious

00:50:54.300 --> 00:50:58.380
since, previously, the
Earth has been a lot warmer?

00:50:58.380 --> 00:51:01.030
And it didn't start to boil.

00:51:01.030 --> 00:51:03.000
WILLIAM HAPPER:
Yeah, absolutely.

00:51:03.000 --> 00:51:06.660
The reason the models
get these huge numbers--

00:51:06.660 --> 00:51:09.390
and mine had the same flaw--

00:51:09.390 --> 00:51:11.940
is because of the
water vapor feedback.

00:51:11.940 --> 00:51:16.890
And I think the reason
that those feedbacks

00:51:16.890 --> 00:51:19.440
are overestimated--

00:51:19.440 --> 00:51:21.310
and I've worked
personally on this,

00:51:21.310 --> 00:51:23.070
so I know a lot about it--

00:51:23.070 --> 00:51:26.760
is that they don't treat clouds
correctly at the same time.

00:51:26.760 --> 00:51:29.700
You can't increase water vapor
without affecting clouds.

00:51:29.700 --> 00:51:33.300
And just a few percent
change in cloudiness

00:51:33.300 --> 00:51:38.173
completely overwhelms any
change from CO2 or water vapor.

00:51:38.173 --> 00:51:39.840
I mean, we're all
familiar with the fact

00:51:39.840 --> 00:51:42.150
that if it's a hot summer
day and a cloud comes over,

00:51:42.150 --> 00:51:43.680
it cools down.

00:51:43.680 --> 00:51:46.410
And if you take that
on a global scale,

00:51:46.410 --> 00:51:50.250
it turns out that there's
just exquisite sensitivity

00:51:50.250 --> 00:51:52.860
to cloudiness.

00:51:52.860 --> 00:51:56.160
Just to follow up on
another allusion you made,

00:51:56.160 --> 00:51:59.070
if there really were
this positive feedback,

00:51:59.070 --> 00:52:02.400
you can't look at the
geological history of the Earth.

00:52:02.400 --> 00:52:05.310
And it could not be as stable.

00:52:05.310 --> 00:52:06.930
The climate could
not be as stable

00:52:06.930 --> 00:52:10.440
as the geological record
indicates if there

00:52:10.440 --> 00:52:11.970
were these positive feedbacks.

00:52:11.970 --> 00:52:16.020
Because the temperature would
be shooting up and down,

00:52:16.020 --> 00:52:18.240
up and down all of
the time, for example,

00:52:18.240 --> 00:52:19.440
because of these changes.

00:52:19.440 --> 00:52:21.270
Solar output, I mentioned.

00:52:21.270 --> 00:52:23.480
But that's not observed.

00:52:23.480 --> 00:52:26.730
The Earth's climate turns
out to be remarkably stable.

00:52:26.730 --> 00:52:30.030
If there are feedbacks,
there are probably

00:52:30.030 --> 00:52:31.980
negative feedbacks.

00:52:31.980 --> 00:52:36.120
And they probably involve
clouds, is my educated guess.

00:52:38.710 --> 00:52:41.470
SPEAKER 2: We have time
for one more question.

00:52:41.470 --> 00:52:44.860
AUDIENCE: I appreciate the
discussion and the knowledge

00:52:44.860 --> 00:52:46.750
that you passed on.

00:52:46.750 --> 00:52:48.700
When I look at this
issue, though, it's

00:52:48.700 --> 00:52:53.890
more about marketing and
perception than science.

00:52:53.890 --> 00:52:56.440
Being retired, I had a
chance to read these studies

00:52:56.440 --> 00:52:57.530
as they're published.

00:52:57.530 --> 00:53:00.580
And I noticed a
little trend where

00:53:00.580 --> 00:53:04.750
they would make a sort
of scientific statement

00:53:04.750 --> 00:53:07.570
about climate change.

00:53:07.570 --> 00:53:09.730
And two or three
sentences later, then they

00:53:09.730 --> 00:53:12.730
go into the projected impacts--

00:53:12.730 --> 00:53:17.920
coastal flooding, deserts
everywhere, and so forth.

00:53:17.920 --> 00:53:22.960
And the media, of course,
only reads the first paragraph

00:53:22.960 --> 00:53:25.390
or whatever, or
whatever they're fed.

00:53:25.390 --> 00:53:27.130
And so then they publish that.

00:53:27.130 --> 00:53:29.770
And it just scares the
bejesus out of people.

00:53:29.770 --> 00:53:33.970
And I have relatives
that believe this stuff.

00:53:33.970 --> 00:53:37.540
And you try and explain
what you're telling us.

00:53:37.540 --> 00:53:39.390
And with new knowledge,
maybe I can do that.

00:53:39.390 --> 00:53:41.140
But you can't get
through to these people.

00:53:41.140 --> 00:53:45.570
They're just scared from the
perception and the marketing.

00:53:45.570 --> 00:53:47.690
So what do we do?

00:53:47.690 --> 00:53:49.940
WILLIAM HAPPER: Well, that's
sort of the key question,

00:53:49.940 --> 00:53:55.580
because the scientific support
for it really doesn't exist.

00:53:55.580 --> 00:53:58.790
But so many people
are scared now.

00:53:58.790 --> 00:54:02.600
And so many people have been
brainwashed since childhood.

00:54:02.600 --> 00:54:06.590
I know I watched our children,
our grandchildren in school.

00:54:06.590 --> 00:54:11.570
And they get
incessant barrage of,

00:54:11.570 --> 00:54:13.650
the world is coming to an end.

00:54:13.650 --> 00:54:15.590
You're unlikely to
finish your life

00:54:15.590 --> 00:54:19.130
because you're going to fry
and because your dad's driving

00:54:19.130 --> 00:54:20.420
an SUV.

00:54:20.420 --> 00:54:26.180
And there are a fair
fraction of kids

00:54:26.180 --> 00:54:31.160
who are sufficiently independent
thinkers in every generation,

00:54:31.160 --> 00:54:32.240
that they laugh this off.

00:54:32.240 --> 00:54:34.862
But there are a lot
who take it seriously.

00:54:34.862 --> 00:54:35.570
And they go home.

00:54:35.570 --> 00:54:36.680
They can't sleep at night.

00:54:36.680 --> 00:54:42.680
You've got Greta Thunberg
having hysterics in front

00:54:42.680 --> 00:54:45.540
of the mighty of the world.

00:54:45.540 --> 00:54:46.790
But she doesn't know anything.

00:54:46.790 --> 00:54:49.663
The amazing thing about Greta--

00:54:49.663 --> 00:54:51.080
those of you who
know a little bit

00:54:51.080 --> 00:54:52.760
about the history
of science, Greta's

00:54:52.760 --> 00:54:57.230
the great granddaughter
of Svante Arrhenius.

00:54:57.230 --> 00:55:00.020
And Svante Arrhenius
was the first person

00:55:00.020 --> 00:55:05.085
to seriously try to calculate
the effects of more CO2.

00:55:05.085 --> 00:55:06.710
He thought it would
be good for Sweden.

00:55:06.710 --> 00:55:09.085
He was a Swedish chemist.

00:55:09.085 --> 00:55:10.710
And it would have
been good for Sweden.

00:55:10.710 --> 00:55:12.410
In fact, it is being
good for Sweden.

00:55:12.410 --> 00:55:16.220
Sweden, for example, is getting
much higher forestry yields now

00:55:16.220 --> 00:55:20.720
than it did 50 years
ago because of more CO2.

00:55:20.720 --> 00:55:26.000
But I think he would be appalled
to see his great granddaughter

00:55:26.000 --> 00:55:31.490
having hysterics in front
of the world for something

00:55:31.490 --> 00:55:33.050
that he was convinced
would be good.

00:55:37.248 --> 00:55:39.290
SPEAKER 3: Please join me
in thanking Dr. Happer.

00:55:39.290 --> 00:55:42.640
[APPLAUSE]

